      "category": "fuel",
      "title": "5.7L Vortec Spider Injector Assembly Failure (CPI)",
      "description": "The 5.7L Vortec V8 (1996-1999) uses a Central Port Injection (CPI) spider assembly with poppet nozzles that clog and leak fuel. The plastic fuel lines become brittle and crack, causing hard starts, rough idle, and fuel odor.",
      "solution": "Confirm the failure by checking fuel pressure and leak-down, then remove the upper intake plenum and inspect the CPI \"spider\" for raw fuel, cracked nylon lines, or sticking poppet nozzles. The permanent repair is to replace the original central port injector assembly with the updated MFI spider conversion kit (GM/ACDelco), along with the upper plenum gasket and, if needed, the fuel pressure regulator; clean any carbon from the intake ports and verify 58-64 psi fuel pressure afterward. This repair typically runs about $350-$700 in parts and labor, or $900+ if a shop also replaces related fuel system components and performs additional intake gasket service.",
      "severity": "high",
      "confidence": "medium",
      "symptoms": [
